{"id":61,"course_id":19,"title":"Modules, Variables, and Environment Promotion","summary":"Design reusable infrastructure without hiding risk.","sequence":2,"body_simplified":"## Mental model\nGood modules expose stable inputs and outputs while keeping provider-specific complexity reviewable. Promotion means using the same module with controlled values, not copy-pasting random stacks.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice comparing dev/stage/prod tfvars, module versions, outputs, and dependency boundaries.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nReview an EKS module call and identify which variables affect security, capacity, and cost.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","body_traditional":"## Mental model\nGood modules expose stable inputs and outputs while keeping provider-specific complexity reviewable. Promotion means using the same module with controlled values, not copy-pasting random stacks.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice comparing dev/stage/prod tfvars, module versions, outputs, and dependency boundaries.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nReview an EKS module call and identify which variables affect security, capacity, and cost.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","pinyin":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-terraform-aws-infrastructure","course_category":"Terraform","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":276,"simplified":"Terraform for AWS Platform Infrastructure","traditional":"Terraform for AWS Platform Infrastructure","pinyin":"Terraform","definition":"Learn reproducible infrastructure workflows for VPCs, EKS modules, state, plans, reviews, drift, and environment promotion."},{"id":277,"simplified":"Evidence","traditional":"Evidence","pinyin":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":278,"simplified":"Blast radius","traditional":"Blast radius","pinyin":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":279,"simplified":"Rollback","traditional":"Rollback","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":280,"simplified":"Runbook","traditional":"Runbook","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":166,"lesson_id":61,"prompt":"What is the main goal of Modules, Variables, and Environment Promotion?","answer":"Design reusable infrastructure without hiding risk.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":167,"lesson_id":61,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":168,"lesson_id":61,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
